          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The responses collectively treat hate as context-dependent: ordinarily hostility, enmity, or moral opposition, but in prominent comparative, discipleship, and election texts as loving less, rejecting, renouncing, or not choosing.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book gives hate a unified volitional and relational sense: to un-choose or repudiate a covenant partner or competing claim, enacted through disfavor, refusal, or expulsion rather than defined primarily by hostile emotion.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Hostility or settled opposition, with a recognized comparative/covenantal sense of rejection or lesser preference in certain contexts.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Volitional repudiation: withdrawing choice, allegiance, or covenantal favor in favor of another claim.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book elevates the recognized rejection/non-election sense into the governing definition and reclassifies comparative preference, discipleship renunciation, and divine disfavor as forms of un-choosing. It correspondingly excludes or must reinterpret cases of interpersonal animosity, moral abhorrence, and worldly hostility that consensus permits as genuine senses of hate without an identifiable covenantal choice or repudiated claim.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no contradiction in the cited comparative and rejection contexts; the book closely tracks a well-recognized consensus reading there. The material disagreement is its apparent reduction of the broader, context-dependent semantic field to volitional repudiation rather than admitting hostility or abhorrence as independent senses.\",\"rationale\":\"The identification is not novel because every consensus response recognizes hate as rejection, non-choice, renunciation, or comparative preference in important passages. It is not a full match because the consensus also recognizes ordinary hostility and moral abhorrence as genuine contextual meanings, whereas the book presents un-choosing as the term's underlying essence. This preserves the consensus category of rejection while materially narrowing and reclassifying its extension.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The selected texts illustrate preference and repudiation well—especially Genesis 29, Proverbs 1, and Hosea 9—but they cannot by themselves establish that all scriptural hatred is fundamentally un-choosing. Texts such as 1 John 2:9–11 and 3:15 appear to concern interpersonal animosity as such and are not naturally reduced to covenantal repudiation.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
